Title:
DigDug

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
DigDug is a Python tool that inflates Windows executables to a target size to evade AV/EDR engines that skip or reduce analysis for large files (commonly cited ~100–150MB thresholds). It appends repeated dictionary words (or optionally random data) to increase file size while avoiding obvious null-byte padding and potentially lowering “suspicious” entropy patterns compared to purely random padding. The repo also integrates SigThief-derived logic to copy an Authenticode signature from a signed “source” executable onto the inflated output. This is primarily useful for red teams and penetration testers testing size/entropy-based scanning gaps and operational constraints in endpoint products. It’s interesting because it targets a pragmatic, non-exploit evasion vector (scanner resource limits/heuristics) and combines it with signature cloning to better blend into trust heuristics.

Technical Focus:
- File-size threshold evasion against AV/EDR static analysis
- Executable padding via dictionary-based content vs null bytes/random data
- Entropy/heuristic considerations in malware scanning
- Authenticode signature copying (SigThief technique)
- Python-based binary manipulation workflows

Use Cases:
- Inflate payloads to test whether specific AV/EDR products enforce size-based scanning limits
- Compare detection differences between dictionary padding, random padding, and null-byte padding
- Clone a signature from a legitimate binary to evaluate trust/allowlisting behaviors in lab environments
- Generate large “benign-looking” test binaries for defensive validation and tuning

Keywords:
DigDug, AV evasion, EDR evasion, file inflation, size threshold, static analysis bypass, entropy, padding, dictionary words, null-byte padding, random padding, Windows executable, PE file, Authenticode, digital signature cloning, SigThief, CrowdStrike Falcon, heuristic detection, red team tooling, Python3